"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2, negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The biopsy revealed a Gleason 3+3 adenocarcinoma, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen's Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 4). No lymph nodes were removed, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The preoperative PSA was 11. The absence of documented nodal sampling means that the status is pNx, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal involvement.\n\nThe documented negative margins and absence of seminal vesicle invasion are favorable prognostic factors. Conversely, the higher preoperative PSA and the higher Gleason grade of the prostatectomy specimen present countervailing findings. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory remain unknown, which is a significant factor limiting prognosis assessment. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory would be crucial to revise this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
